      "writtenOn": "2026-03-10T17:25:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The ABI tooling does not magically know which types are abstract (a.k.a. opaque). The notion of abstract could even vary depending on the subsystem. Tooling here means the low-level tool, `stg`, and the Kleaf scripting around it.\n\nI have commented more about this in the linked bug.\n\nBTF does not have hashes of function types. BTF is a symbol / type graph. The DWARF processor that creates BTF information for the kernel also does not know which types are abstract.\n\nMODVERSIONS CRCs *are* generated by hashing strings extracted from yet another graph of symbol / type information. This graph exists ephemerally within `genksyms` or `gendwarfksyms` and can be exported as `symtypes`.\n\nMODVERSIONS CRCs are checked by the module loader and are picked up by STG and added to the ABI representation. They are not part of the kernel BTF.",
      "parentUuid": "5c86d34d_0a42f3b4",
      "revId": "df46a087fa0f2efaa72d630c9c42353e72e45fc8",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
